## CLASSIFICATION CRITERIA

complexity = "trivial" when the goal is a SINGLE self-contained deliverable an agent can produce in one turn with no external research, no new specialist, and no multi-step plan:
- "a list of 10 birds", "a haiku about the sea", "a one-page markdown summary of X"
- "a sample JSON config", "a short outline", "rename these three things"
- Anything where the answer IS the deliverable and it fits in one writing pass.

complexity = "moderate" when the goal needs a few clear steps or one existing specialist, but no research, no training, and no multi-phase program:
- "compare these two documents and write up the differences"
- "draft a project plan from this brief"
- "summarize across the files already in my knowledge base"

complexity = "complex" when the goal is a multi-phase program, needs multiple specialists, requires external research/web fetches, or implies building a durable reused capability:
- "build me a competitive-analysis report researching the top 5 vendors"
- "set up an ongoing weekly digest of my calendar and email"
- "create a specialist that can do X going forward"

## RULES
- When in doubt between trivial and moderate, prefer the CHEAPER class (trivial) -- a misclassified trivial goal that turns out to need more simply produces a first pass; a misclassified moderate goal needlessly burns the expensive loop.
- A goal that merely sounds long but is still one deliverable ("a detailed 2-page essay on photosynthesis") is TRIVIAL -- length is not complexity.
- Mentioning a tool the agent already has (write a file, make a list) is NOT complexity.

